---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Working through your path — apprenti.dev Docs">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold">
                Working through your path
            </h1>
        </div>

        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-8">
            <section>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Three tabs carry most of your day: <strong
                        >Today</strong
                    >, <strong>Path</strong>, and the task itself.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Today</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    <strong>Today</strong> shows the one thing that matters
                    right now: your current task, a <strong
                        >Continue</strong
                    > action, and roughly how much effort is left. It also
                    surfaces anything waiting on someone else — mentor
                    feedback you haven't read, or a revision request —
                    without dumping your entire multi-year plan on the
                    screen every time you open the app.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Path</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    <strong>Path</strong> is the hierarchical view of the
                    whole apprenticeship: year → term → module → task. Each
                    task shows one status:
                </p>
                <ul class="space-y-1 text-muted-foreground mt-2">
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                        <span class="text-link mt-1">•</span>Not started
                    </li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                        <span class="text-link mt-1">•</span>In progress
                    </li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                        <span class="text-link mt-1">•</span>Submitted
                    </li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                        <span class="text-link mt-1">•</span>Revision
                        requested
                    </li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                        <span class="text-link mt-1">•</span>Approved
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mt-4">
                    Most curricula are <strong>sequential</strong> — you
                    submit earlier tasks before later ones become available
                    for submission (you can still read ahead). A curriculum
                    may instead allow <strong>random</strong> order. You can
                    optionally overlay competency tags on the Path to see
                    which tasks build which skills.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Task detail</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Opening a task lays out, in order: <strong
                        >Objective</strong
                    >, <strong>Why this matters</strong>, <strong
                        >Resources</strong
                    >, <strong>Instructions</strong>, <strong
                        >Evidence</strong
                    > requirements, <strong>Acceptance criteria</strong>,
                    your <strong>Notes</strong>, your <strong
                        >Reflection</strong
                    >, <strong>AI assistance</strong>, <strong
                        >Submission</strong
                    >, mentor feedback, and history. You don't need to hunt
                    for what's expected — it's all on one screen.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Notes</h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Add a note at any point — your own thought, or a quoted
                    passage from the instructions (select text, then <strong
                        >Create note</strong
                    >). Notes are plain Markdown files in your fork, so
                    they're yours permanently, and you can link between them
                    and tasks with <code>[[note:id]]</code> / <code
                        >[[task:y1t1-002]]</code
                    > style references.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">
                    Logging work
                </h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Use <strong>Log work</strong> to record time against a
                    task (<code>1h</code>, <code>30m</code>, <code
                        >1h 30m</code
                    > — up to 24h per entry, with an optional comment on
                    what you did). <strong>Time logged</strong> on the task
                    shows your running total, optionally next to the task's
                    estimated effort. This is for your own record and your
                    mentor's context, not a productivity score.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">
                    Saving vs. submitting
                </h2>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                    Reflections and notes autosave locally as you type, but
                    nothing is durable — nothing survives a fresh install or
                    reaches your mentor — until you explicitly <strong
                        >Save reflection</strong
                    > / <strong>Save work</strong>, which commits it to your
                    fork. Submitting for review is a separate, deliberate
                    action, covered in <a
                        href="/docs/apprentice-submitting-evidence"
                        class="text-link hover:underline"
                        >Submitting evidence for review</a
                    >.
                </p>
            </section>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 flex gap-4">
            <a href="/docs" class="text-sm text-link hover:underline"
                >← All Docs</a
            >
            <a href="/" class="text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:underline"
                >Back to apprenti.dev</a
            >
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
